[Music]  Purdue Pharma laid off its entire sales  team this week now only on CBS this  morning  a former Purdue sales rep claims the  drug maker downplayed the dangers of  opioids even after pleading guilty to a  felony charge of misbranding Purdue's  2007 settlement with the Justice  Department included more than 630  million dollars in fines but 24 states  and more than 400 cities and counties  are suing Purdue and its competitors  accusing them of fuelling an opioid drug  crisis that's killed more than 200,000  people Tony de copal is here with our  CBS News investigation Tony good morning  good morning big numbers and really  serious accusations Purdue makes  oxycotin you've probably heard of it too  perhaps the best-known opioid painkiller  in America in 2007 the company admitted  the falsely selling the drug as less  addictive than rivals but now a former  Purdue salesperson tells CBS News the  deceptive sales did not end there it was  always in the back of my mind that maybe  the company had not told us the whole  truth when they hired us when we  interviewed when we went through  training this is our class Carol Panera  trained for a sales job with Purdue  Pharma in 2008 so this was for the  quarter not bad  she showed us how the company's bonus  system put her in a position to make  more money by convincing doctors to  prescribe higher doses of oxycontin for  a wider range of pain  so bottom line sell as much as you can  sell as much as you can the idea being  that we're trying to expand our reach  beyond just pain doctors a year earlier  Purdue had admitted it falsely promoted  oxy cotton as less addictive by among  other means claiming the drugs  slow-release formula did not cause a  buzz or euphoria and could be used to  weed out addicts the company said the  misstatements ended in 2001 they made it  sound like it was a little bit of a  witch hunt on the government's part  but amid skyrocketing addiction rates  and overdoses related to oxycontin  panera claims the company taught a sales  tactic she now considers questionable  saying some patients might only appear  to be addicted when in fact they're just  in pain in training she was taught a  term for this pseudo addiction so the  cure for pseudo addiction you were  trained is more opioids a higher dose  yes did this concept of pseudo addiction  come with studies backing it up we had  no studies we actually we did not have  any studies that's the thing that was  kind of disturbing was that we didn't  have studies to present to the doctors  you know that sounds I know I was naive  a 2015 study published in current  addiction reports found no empirical  evidence to support pseudo addiction as  a diagnosis in a statement to CBS News  Purdue said it's confident that it's  past marketing and sales of its  prescription opioid medications have  been consistent with the information  contained in the FDA approved label but  the word pseudo addiction doesn't appear  on oxycottons label and a spokesperson  for the FDA said the labeling is not  intended as a discussion of pseudo  addiction a big loss real easy to  explain you know you just come up with a  ridiculous term like pseudo addiction  Oklahoma Attorney General Mike hunter is  one of two dozen AG's suing Purdue and  other opioid manufacturers many of the  lawsuits mentioned pseudo addiction and  that maybe it's the most disgraceful  conduct that we we've seen in the whole  pattern of disgraceful conduct so as far  as you can tell there is no scientific  basis for a pseudo addiction I reject  any notion that there's science behind  pseudo addiction hunter alleges that  Purdue and its competitors have caused a  devastating public health crisis costing  his state billions of dollars a year do  you think this is an epidemic that  begins with Purdue Pharma and oxycontin  yes that's the start of it all yes  Purdue has reportedly been in  discussions with multiple states for a  global settlement of lawsuits but hunter  says he's not interested in joining  those talks I have no confidence in that  process  I've confidence in our lawyers I have  confidence in our laws I've confidence  in a jury of Oklahomans  Carol panera who quit in 2013 says she  hopes the drug maker is held accountable  I think they misrepresented to the  public I think they misrepresented to  their salespeople and yeah I think it  was just a big charade to what end  - making money - making money  do you regret the five years you spent  with Purdue Pharma I'm gonna sum it up  and say this I think that was one of the  if not the worst career decisions of my  life in February the company said it  would stop training sales people to sell  oxycontin and this week it announced it  would stop making sales calls for the  rest of its drugs produced as its  diversifying beyond pain medications  working to develop new drugs to treat  cancer and central nervous system  disorders Thank You Carol Burnett for  speaking up pseudo addiction sounds like  a made-up word to hide very questionable  business practices do you mean  potentially a very damaging one if you  look at the sales of oxycontin they went  down in 2007-2008 and then they tripled  right after Carol took the job thank you  Tony great piece

